      "after": "A challenge names the assumption or the cost it attacks. When a mind is challenged, its next turn answers that challenge: it shows why the objection fails, or it gives up the part of its position the objection hit. Repeating its opening position is not an answer.\n\nBy the end of the debate, at least one mind holds a different position from the one it opened with, because of something another mind said. The turn where it changes names the argument that moved it and says what it now recommends or claims instead. Agreeing while keeping the same recommendation is not a change of position.",
      "reason": "The six principle bullets are filled by rote: the concession bullet produces an 'I concede ... but' tick at T7 in five of ten transcripts with the position unchanged, and objections are met by restatement. Two paragraphs of content rules say what an answer and a change of position must contain, which this worker follows, and cut section 4's instruction count."
